  "body": "PR1638 proves three reciprocal guard bits suffice but leaves open whether its **actual seeded routine** works with two. This contribution settles that question: with the unchanged seed 5/8, six-bit warm-up, floor rounding, exact products and capped doubling, target p=15 and c=127379/65536 return x=67435/131072. The residual exceeds 2^-16 by exactly 655/8589934592.\n\n**Status: scoped mathematical result; no new kappa.** The complete reachable trace and an exact interval of failing inputs are proved in the README. Actual seeded failures also exclude zero and one guard. Combined with the explicitly credited PR1638 upper proof, three is therefore the least uniform nonnegative integer guard count for this fixed residual contract. All earlier stages satisfy their bounds. This is not a counterexample to PR1638's theorem, a minimum failing-precision claim, or optimality for arbitrary schedules or absolute-error-only approximations.\n\nThe proof is short enough to check directly. An integer-significand evaluator corroborates the witnesses. A separate rational certificate checker validates floor inequalities, row connections and a schedule derived from powers of two; it does not execute the recurrence or import the evaluator. This avoids treating two arithmetic representations with a shared traversal as independent validation.\n\nReproduce offline:\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/reciprocal-seeded-guard-boundary/verify.py\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_SEEDED_GUARD_BOUNDARY witnesses=3 controls=7; no new kappa`.\n\nLocal validation on stable CPython 3.15.0 passes:\n\n- Three exact reachable witnesses, six positive runs using three/five guards, and seven mathematical rejection controls.\n- Read-only relocation and four actual source/certificate corruptions, including coherently rehashed invalid certificates.\n- Source/task metadata, selected baseline bindings, five selected-record tests, and 19 workflow tests. One workflow test requires Python 3.12 and is skipped.\n\nThe opt-in replay manifest targets the repository's Python 3.12 CI environment; local registered-3.12 execution and hosted CI are unclaimed. No independent human review, proof-kernel theorem, complete root/recovery substitution, physical-tape admission, performance result, or selected cold replay is claimed. Section09 is a downstream arithmetic corollary, not a dependency of the multiplication theorem.\n\nOnly `research/reciprocal-seeded-guard-boundary/` is added. Assignment seed63, base `1f7a934975e5eacaf83511c9ac870263bf095e05`; submission parent `abf8fa57544ebf8f5029120ee0552418f584893b`. The source pins are unchanged across that refresh. The final overlap snapshot covers 535 open PRs and 296 discussion records, both fully paginated without warnings. PR1638 remains at `394b90ee7fdaedd1f666a78a442127be3da17810`; Discussion1634 releases its claim and explicitly identifies this question. Text coverage is not a priority guarantee. No pinned coordination issue exists; the user-authorized claim and handoff are local.\n\nCredit **@ToikY / PR1638** for the three-guard upper theorem, **Hanson Sun (@bbMisk) / PR1528** for the preceding bounded Newton audit, and the retained OpenAI manuscript/classical Brent reduction. Unchanged predecessor proof and README copies retain their original disclosures. New work is Apache-2.0, prepared for **Charles Bethin (@cbethin) with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance** in derivation, implementation, checking and writing.\n",
